<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad ibn Said al-Busaidi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1783">1783</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ahmad bin Said</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad ibn Said al-Busaidi was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-said-dynasty">Al Bu Said dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sohar">Sohar</a> in the 1740s he resisted the Persian forces drawn in by the Ya'aruba civil war, drove them out and was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. His family went on to rule Oman and, until 1964, Zanzibar.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmad bin Said was born in 1694 in the city of Adam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, the son of Said bin Ahmad al-Busaidi. His family, the Al Bu Said, were a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinawi">Hinawi</a> tribe from the interior of Oman. It was said that he began as a wood vendor and worked his way up; he became a leading merchant of the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sohar">Sohar</a>, and in 1737 he was appointed its governor. Persian troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, called in by the Yaruba Imam Saif bin Sultan II, had begun to conquer the country, and after a nine-month siege of Sohar Ahmad negotiated an honourable surrender and was confirmed as governor in return for tribute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>By late 1744 Ahmad controlled large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. Bal'arab bin Himyar of the Yaruba had been elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> in 1743 and kept the support of part of the Ghafiri. Ahmad was deserted by his troops at the Battle of Bithnah around the start of 1745 and had to flee. He then delayed paying tribute, so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> could not pay their troops and many deserted. In 1747, according to the 19th-century Omani historian Salil Ibn Raziq, Ahmad invited the Persian officers to a banquet at his fort in Barka and had them killed. Other accounts say the Persians withdrew voluntarily.</p><p>In the second half of 1749 Ahmad defeated and killed Bal'arab near Jebel Akhdar, ending Yaruba power. On 9 June 1749 the Ibadi tribes elected him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rustaq">Rustaq</a>, where he made his seat. He ruled for 39 generally peaceful years. He encouraged agriculture and maritime trade, won tribal leaders over through commercial ventures, and kept a permanent army and navy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 1 February 1775 Ahmad handed routine administration to his son Said bin Ahmad, keeping the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a>. In 1781 his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> and Saif rebelled and took the forts of al-Mirani and al-Jalali at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, and imprisoned Said. Ahmad returned to Muscat, recaptured al-Mirani and regained al-Jalali by bombardment. He died on 15 December 1783 at Al-Batinah Fort in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rustaq">Rustaq</a> and was buried there near the Great Mosque. Said bin Ahmad was elected Imam in his place, though he soon abdicated while keeping the title. After that the dynasty's members were sultans of Muscat, with only limited authority over the interior until 1959. Ahmad was the only ruler of his line who was clearly an Imam, elected in the traditional way.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmad bin Said al-Busaidi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1740s <b>Ahmad ibn Said</b>, governor of Sohar, expelled the Persian forces that had intervened in the civil war of the Ya'aruba dynasty and was elected imam.</blockquote><a href="/state/omani-empire">Omani Empire</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ahmad ibn Said</b> of Sohar expelled the Persians and was elected imam around 1744, founding the Omani Empire of the Al Bu Said.</blockquote><a href="/state/yaaruba-dynasty">Ya'aruba dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
